  <!-- ================= MICROBORE ================= -->
  <section class="pfx pfx-sec pfx-sec--paper" id="microbore">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="pfx-eyebrow">The one most firms turn down</div>
      <h2>Do you flush microbore central heating systems?</h2>
      <div class="pfx-water pfx-water--sm"></div>
      <p class="pfx-lede">Yes. A microbore powerflush is a different job to a standard one. Microbore pipework is 8mm to 10mm across and blocks far more easily. It needs high flow pumps, double dosed cleaner and flow reversal to clear properly.</p>
      <p class="pfx-lede">Microbore powerflush Liverpool is one of the few searches where people are looking for a specialist rather than the cheapest quote, and they are right to. Plenty of engineers turn these systems down.</p>

      <table class="ct">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Comparison</th><th scope="col">Microbore</th><th scope="col">Standard</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Pipe size</th><td data-label="Microbore">8mm to 10mm</td><td data-label="Standard">15mm or 22mm</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Typical build years</th><td data-label="Microbore">1985 to 2000</td><td data-label="Standard">Most other periods</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">How fast it blocks</th><td data-label="Microbore">Quickly. Narrow bore fills with iron oxide</td><td data-label="Standard"><span class="ct-win">Slowly</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">What clearing it needs</th><td data-label="Microbore">High flow, double dosed cleaner, flow reversal</td><td data-label="Standard"><span class="ct-win">Standard flush technique</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Time on site</th><td data-label="Microbore">Longer. Sometimes much longer</td><td data-label="Standard"><span class="ct-win">Predictable</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">How we price it</th><td data-label="Microbore">At survey, once we have seen it</td><td data-label="Standard"><span class="ct-win">Fixed by radiator count</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="pfx-note">
        <strong>How to tell if you have microbore</strong>
        <p>Look at the pipe going into a radiator valve. Microbore is noticeably thinner than normal copper pipe, thin enough to bend by hand. It is often plastic coated and usually runs from a central manifold rather than looping round the house.</p>
        <p>Plenty of Liverpool homes have it, particularly the semis built through the late eighties and nineties around Childwall, Woolton and Gateacre. If your heating has been patchy since you moved in and one or two radiators have never worked, microbore sludge is a strong suspect.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pfx-note">
        <strong>Why we survey instead of quoting</strong>
        <p>Two microbore systems can look identical and take completely different amounts of work. One clears in a morning. The next has a fully blocked leg that needs the flow reversing repeatedly and takes all day.</p>
        <p>Quoting blind would mean overcharging the easy ones or walking off the hard ones. We would rather look first and give you a real number.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= MAGNETIC FILTER ================= -->
  <section class="pfx pfx-sec pfx-sec--mist" id="filter">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="pfx-eyebrow">Optional, £195 fitted</div>
      <h2>Do I need a magnetic filter after a power flush?</h2>
      <p class="pfx-lede">Not compulsory, but it is the best way to keep the system clean afterwards. We fit a MagnaClean Professional2 for £195 including VAT at the same visit.</p>

      <div class="pfx-note">
        <strong>What it actually does</strong>
        <p>A flush clears the sludge that is in there now. It does not stop new iron oxide forming, because that is what happens when water sits against steel. Inhibitor slows it down. A magnetic filter catches what still forms, before it settles in your radiators and boiler.</p>
        <p>The filter sits on the return pipe near the boiler. You empty it once a year at the service. The first time you open it after a year is usually the moment people understand why it was worth fitting.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pfx-note">
        <strong>Fitted on the day, or not at all</strong>
        <p>Fitting one during the flush costs £195. Fitting one later as its own visit costs more, because it means a trip out and a system drain down on its own. If you are going to have one, have it done on the day.</p>
        <p>Some boiler warranties now require a filter to stay valid. Worth checking yours before you decide.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= CHEMICALS ================= -->
  <section class="pfx pfx-sec pfx-sec--paper" id="chemicals">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="pfx-eyebrow">Decided on the day, not in advance</div>
      <h2>Which cleaning chemical do you use in a power flush?</h2>
      <div class="pfx-water pfx-water--sm"></div>
      <p class="pfx-lede">Fernox F3 on standard cleans and Sentinel X400 on heavily sludged systems. We choose after we see the water, not before.</p>
      <p class="pfx-lede">Central heating cleaning services vary far more than the price suggests. The chemical is where most of that difference sits, and almost nobody tells you which one they are putting in your system.</p>

      <table class="ct">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Comparison</th><th scope="col">Sentinel X400</th><th scope="col">Fernox F3</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">What it is for</th><td data-label="Sentinel X400">Heavy sludge and years of corrosion</td><td data-label="Fernox F3">Standard cleans and most flushes</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">How it works</th><td data-label="Sentinel X400">A restorer. Breaks down dense iron oxide over time</td><td data-label="Fernox F3">A fast universal cleaner. Works during the flush</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Time it needs</th><td data-label="Sentinel X400">Can circulate for up to 3 to 4 weeks</td><td data-label="Fernox F3"><span class="ct-win">Usually done inside an hour</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Finished same day</th><td data-label="Sentinel X400">Not always. Bad systems may need a return visit</td><td data-label="Fernox F3"><span class="ct-win">Yes</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">When we pick it</th><td data-label="Sentinel X400">Black water, blocked rads, years of neglect</td><td data-label="Fernox F3">Cold spots, mild to moderate sludge</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="pfx-note">
        <strong>Why this matters to you</strong>
        <p>Most firms use one chemical for every job because it is simpler. If your system is badly sludged, a fast cleaner will not shift it and you have paid for a flush that did not fix the problem.</p>
        <p>If we open your system and find heavy sludge, we will say that X400 needs time to work. That can mean dosing the system and coming back. It is slower, and it is the only way to actually clear it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

