  <!-- ================= PROCESS ================= -->
  <section class="pfx pfx-sec pfx-sec--paper" id="process">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="pfx-eyebrow">Seven stages</div>
      <h2>What happens during a power flush?</h2>
      <div class="pfx-water pfx-water--sm"></div>
      <p class="pfx-lede">From arriving to handing the system back. Most of a day for a standard Liverpool semi. This is what a proper central heating power flush involves, start to finish.</p>

      <div class="pr">
        <div class="pr-step"><div class="pr-num">1</div><div><strong>System check</strong><p>James looks at the boiler, pipework and radiators, then bleeds one radiator to see the water. Black water means heavy sludge, and that changes which chemical goes in.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pr-step"><div class="pr-num">2</div><div><strong>Machine connected</strong><p>The Kamco CF90 Quantum2 goes on, usually at the pump head or across a radiator tail. It is tank mounted, so it comes in on a trolley rather than through the house in pieces.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pr-step"><div class="pr-num">3</div><div><strong>Cleaner added</strong><p>Fernox F3 for a standard clean. Sentinel X400 where the sludge is heavy. This is where a lot of the result is decided.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pr-step"><div class="pr-num">4</div><div><strong>Every radiator flushed</strong><p>Each one isolated and flushed on its own, with the flow reversed to shift settled sludge. Stubborn radiators get agitated. This is the slow part, and the part cheap quotes skip.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pr-step"><div class="pr-num">5</div><div><strong>Whole system flushed</strong><p>The system runs until the water comes back clear. Not nearly clear. Clear.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pr-step"><div class="pr-num">6</div><div><strong>Inhibitor added</strong><p>A full dose to BS 7593. Without it the system starts silting up within months and the flush was money wasted.</p></div></div>
        <div class="pr-step"><div class="pr-num">7</div><div><strong>Balanced and reported</strong><p>Radiators balanced so they heat evenly, then a written report for your boiler warranty.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= HONEST ================= -->
  <section class="pfx pfx-sec pfx-sec--mist" id="honest">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="pfx-eyebrow">When we will tell you not to bother</div>
      <h2>Is a power flush always the right fix?</h2>
      <p class="pfx-lede">No. It fixes sludge. It does not fix broken parts, leaks, or a system at the end of its life.</p>

      <div class="vd">
        <div class="vd-row"><div class="vd-mark vd-no">&#10007;</div><div><strong>Your boiler has a broken part</strong><p>A stuck diverter valve, failed pump or dead PCB will not be fixed by a flush. If your symptoms point at a part, we say so and price the repair instead.</p></div></div>
        <div class="vd-row"><div class="vd-mark vd-no">&#10007;</div><div><strong>The system is losing pressure</strong><p>That is a leak. A flush will not seal it and can make it worse. The leak needs finding first.</p></div></div>
        <div class="vd-row"><div class="vd-mark vd-no">&#10007;</div><div><strong>The boiler is past saving</strong><p>Over 15 years old and badly corroded, spending on a flush can be throwing good money after bad. Sometimes replacement is the honest answer.</p></div></div>
        <div class="vd-row"><div class="vd-mark vd-no">&#10007;</div><div><strong>Radiators are corroded through</strong><p>A flush moves water at speed. A radiator already rotten can spring a pin hole. It was going to fail anyway, but you should know before we start, not after.</p></div></div>
        <div class="vd-row"><div class="vd-mark vd-yes">&#10003;</div><div><strong>Cold at the bottom, warm at the top</strong><p>The classic sludge signature. Sludge settles in the bottom and hot water flows over it. Exactly what a flush fixes.</p></div></div>
        <div class="vd-row"><div class="vd-mark vd-yes">&#10003;</div><div><strong>Black water when you bleed a rad</strong><p>That black is iron oxide, and it is circulating through your boiler right now. A flush is the right call.</p></div></div>
        <div class="vd-row"><div class="vd-mark vd-yes">&#10003;</div><div><strong>Banging or kettling boiler</strong><p>Usually restricted flow through the heat exchanger. Clearing it is cheaper than replacing the boiler.</p></div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="pfx-note">
        <strong>What a flush will not do</strong>
        <p>It will not remove every last trace of sludge. Nothing does. It will not turn a failing system into a new one. What it does is clear the blockage, get the radiators heating properly and buy the system years it would not otherwise have had. If anyone promises more than that, be careful.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

